```apex
/**
 * Keeps Account.FB_Open_Pipeline__c and Account.FB_Open_Opportunities__c equal to the sum of
 * Amount and the count of the account's open (IsClosed = false) opportunities.
 *
 * Design:
 * - Totals are recalculated from the database (GROUP BY aggregate) for every affected account,
 *   not applied as deltas. Each after-trigger run sees the data as it currently is inside the
 *   transaction, so totals stay correct across several DML statements on the same records,
 *   undelete, account moves and 200-record loads.
 * - Accounts are only updated when a total actually changes. This keeps the legacy AccountTrigger
 *   (which fires on every Account update) from running needlessly and ends the re-entry loop
 *   Account update -> AccountTrigger -> Opportunity update -> this handler.
 * - Only opportunities whose AccountId, Amount or IsClosed changed trigger a recalculation.
 *   Changes to other fields (for example the rating written by AccountTrigger) are ignored.
 * - Runs without sharing: the totals must include every open opportunity of the account,
 *   not only those visible to the running user.
 */
public without sharing class OpportunityPipelineHandler {

    /**
     * @param records  Trigger.new for insert/update/undelete, Trigger.old for delete.
     * @param oldMap   Trigger.oldMap for update, null otherwise.
     */
    public static void handle(List<Opportunity> records, Map<Id, Opportunity> oldMap) {
        Set<Id> accountIds = collectAffectedAccountIds(records, oldMap);
        if (accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }
        recalculate(accountIds);
    }

    private static Set<Id> collectAffectedAccountIds(List<Opportunity> records, Map<Id, Opportunity> oldMap) {
        Set<Id> accountIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Opportunity opp : records) {
            Opportunity prior = (oldMap == null) ? null : oldMap.get(opp.Id);

            if (prior == null) {
                // Insert, undelete or delete: the record's own account is affected.
                addIfPresent(accountIds, opp.AccountId);
                continue;
            }

            if (prior.AccountId != opp.AccountId) {
                // Moved to another account: both the losing and the gaining account change.
                addIfPresent(accountIds, prior.AccountId);
                addIfPresent(accountIds, opp.AccountId);
            } else if (prior.Amount != opp.Amount || prior.IsClosed != opp.IsClosed) {
                // Amount changed, or a move between open and closed (IsClosed flips).
                addIfPresent(accountIds, opp.AccountId);
            }
        }
        return accountIds;
    }

    private static void addIfPresent(Set<Id> ids, Id accountId) {
        if (accountId != null) {
            ids.add(accountId);
        }
    }

    private static void recalculate(Set<Id> accountIds) {
        // Rating is queried on purpose: AccountTrigger (legacy) reads Account.Rating from
        // Trigger.newMap on every Account update. Updating a partial Account record would
        // leave Rating null there and blank FB_Account_Rating__c on the open opportunities.
        Map<Id, Account> accountsById = new Map<Id, Account>([
            SELECT Id, Rating, FB_Open_Pipeline__c, FB_Open_Opportunities__c
            FROM Account
            WHERE Id IN :accountIds
        ]);
        if (accountsById.isEmpty()) {
            // Accounts no longer exist (for example, deleted in the same transaction).
            return;
        }

        // One aggregate query for all affected accounts. Accounts without open opportunities
        // do not appear in the result and are treated as 0 below.
        Map<Id, Decimal> pipelineByAccount = new Map<Id, Decimal>();
        Map<Id, Integer> countByAccount = new Map<Id, Integer>();
        for (AggregateResult ar : [
            SELECT AccountId, SUM(Amount) pipelineSum, COUNT(Id) oppCount
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds
              AND IsClosed = false
            GROUP BY AccountId
        ]) {
            Id acctId = (Id) ar.get('AccountId');
            Decimal pipelineSum = (Decimal) ar.get('pipelineSum');
            if (pipelineSum == null) {
                // SUM ignores null amounts; if every amount is null the result is null.
                pipelineSum = 0;
            }
            pipelineByAccount.put(acctId, pipelineSum);
            countByAccount.put(acctId, (Integer) ar.get('oppCount'));
        }

        List<Account> toUpdate = new List<Account>();
        for (Account acc : accountsById.values()) {
            Decimal newPipeline = pipelineByAccount.get(acc.Id);
            if (newPipeline == null) {
                newPipeline = 0;
            }
            Integer newCount = countByAccount.get(acc.Id);
            if (newCount == null) {
                newCount = 0;
            }

            Decimal oldPipeline = acc.FB_Open_Pipeline__c;
            if (oldPipeline == null) {
                oldPipeline = 0;
            }
            Integer oldCount = (acc.FB_Open_Opportunities__c == null)
                ? 0
                : acc.FB_Open_Opportunities__c.intValue();

            if (oldPipeline == newPipeline && oldCount == newCount) {
                continue;
            }

            acc.FB_Open_Pipeline__c = newPipeline;
            acc.FB_Open_Opportunities__c = newCount;
            toUpdate.add(acc);
        }

        if (!toUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            update toUpdate;
        }
    }
}
```

Notes on the design:

- **Recalculation from the database.** Each after-trigger run aggregates the open opportunities as they currently stand in the transaction, so the totals are right after every DML, including Flow followed by Apex or repeated DML on the same records. An account with no open opportunities gets 0 and 0.
- **Legacy AccountTrigger.** It reads `Rating` from `Trigger.newMap`, so the accounts are queried with `Rating` and updated as full objects. A minimal `Account(Id, ...)` update would blank `FB_Account_Rating__c` on open opportunities. Accounts are only written when a total changes, which stops the account and opportunity triggers from re-entering each other.
- **Bulk.** A 200-record load costs one Account query, one aggregate query and at most one Account update, with no SOQL or DML inside loops.
- **Sharing.** The handler runs `without sharing` because the totals must count every open opportunity, not just those the running user can see.
- **Known limitation.** A client that writes these two fields from a stale copy of an Account can overwrite the computed values. Keep these fields out of user-facing and integration update paths.

