```apex
/**
 * Pushes newly created Accounts to the ERP as customers.
 *
 * The trigger hands the new Account Ids to a Queueable, which performs the
 * callout asynchronously. Each HTTP request carries up to 200 customers, and
 * every customer carries its own salesforceId so the ERP can trace each
 * account individually.
 */
public with sharing class ErpCustomerSync implements Queueable, Database.AllowsCallouts {

    private static final String ENDPOINT = 'callout:ERP/api/customers';
    private static final Integer MAX_CUSTOMERS_PER_REQUEST = 200;

    private final List<Id> accountIds;

    public ErpCustomerSync(List<Id> accountIds) {
        this.accountIds = accountIds;
    }

    /**
     * Enqueues the sync for the given Account Ids. Does nothing when the set is empty.
     */
    public static void enqueue(Set<Id> newAccountIds) {
        if (newAccountIds == null || newAccountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }
        System.enqueueJob(new ErpCustomerSync(new List<Id>(newAccountIds)));
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {
        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        List<Account> accounts = [
            SELECT Id, Name, BillingCountry
            FROM Account
            WHERE Id IN :accountIds
            WITH USER_MODE
        ];
        Map<Id, Account> accountsById = new Map<Id, Account>(accounts);

        List<ErpCustomer> customers = new List<ErpCustomer>();
        for (Id accountId : accountIds) {
            Account acc = accountsById.get(accountId);
            if (acc == null) {
                continue;
            }
            ErpCustomer customer = new ErpCustomer();
            customer.salesforceId = String.valueOf(acc.Id);
            customer.name = acc.Name;
            customer.country = String.isBlank(acc.BillingCountry) ? null : acc.BillingCountry;
            customers.add(customer);
        }

        for (Integer i = 0; i < customers.size(); i += MAX_CUSTOMERS_PER_REQUEST) {
            Integer lastIndex = Math.min(i + MAX_CUSTOMERS_PER_REQUEST, customers.size());
            sendChunk(customers.subList(i, lastIndex));
        }
    }

    private static void sendChunk(List<ErpCustomer> chunk) {
        HttpRequest req = new HttpRequest();
        req.setEndpoint(ENDPOINT);
        req.setMethod('POST');
        req.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');
        req.setBody(JSON.serialize(chunk, false));

        HttpResponse res = new Http().send(req);

        if (res.getStatusCode() != 202) {
            System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                'ERP customer sync failed. Status: ' + res.getStatusCode() +
                ', customers in request: ' + chunk.size());
        }
    }

    /**
     * Request body element. Property names match the ERP API contract.
     */
    public class ErpCustomer {
        public String salesforceId;
        public String name;
        public String country;
    }
}
```

**Why this differs from the brief**

The brief asks for one callout per new account, made directly from the trigger. Neither works in Apex:

- **Synchronous callouts from triggers are not allowed.** Calling out from the trigger context throws an exception. The callout has to run asynchronously, so I used a Queueable that implements `Database.AllowsCallouts`. This also means an ERP outage cannot block or roll back account creation for users.
- **One request per account breaks at bulk volume.** A batch of 200 accounts would need 200 callouts, and Apex allows 100 per transaction. The ERP API already accepts an array of up to 200 customers, so each trigger batch becomes a single request.
- **Traceability is preserved.** Every customer in the request body has its own `salesforceId`, so the ERP can still trace each account individually. Only the HTTP envelope is shared.

**Other notes**

- The Queueable receives Ids and re-queries Name and BillingCountry. Serialising sObjects into the job is fragile, and the re-query means the payload reflects the record's current state.
- The query uses `WITH USER_MODE`, so the sync respects the running user's field and record access.
- Each trigger invocation enqueues one job. Apex allows 50 queueable jobs per synchronous transaction, so a single DML of more than about 10,000 accounts would hit that limit. Bulk API loads, which run in 200-record transactions, are not affected.
- Non-202 responses are logged with the status code and customer count only. The body is not logged, to avoid writing customer data to debug logs. Retry and error handling are left out, as the ticket specifies.

I batched the publish into a single `EventBus.publish(List)` call instead of calling it inside the loop. Each `EventBus.publish` call counts against a per-transaction publish limit (150 in Apex), so 200 individual calls from a full trigger batch would hit that limit and throw a `LimitException`. `EventBus.publish` on a list returns one `SaveResult` per event in the same order as the input, so the per-record failure mapping you wanted is preserved. Result `i` corresponds to `opportunities[i]`.

