```apex
/**
 * When an Account's BillingCountry changes, propagate the new value to that
 * Account's contacts whose MailingCountry is blank or still equal to the old
 * BillingCountry. Contacts with any other MailingCountry are left untouched.
 *
 * Declared 'without sharing' so that automation updates every related contact
 * regardless of the editing user's record access. Contact-level FLS is not
 * relevant because this is system-driven data maintenance on a single field.
 */
public without sharing class AccountCountrySync {

    public static void syncContactCountries(List<Account> newAccounts, Map<Id, Account> oldAccountsById) {
        Map<Id, String> newCountryByAccountId = new Map<Id, String>();
        Map<Id, String> oldCountryByAccountId = new Map<Id, String>();
        Set<String> oldCountries = new Set<String>();

        // 1. Only accounts whose BillingCountry actually changed.
        for (Account acc : newAccounts) {
            Account oldAcc = oldAccountsById.get(acc.Id);
            if (oldAcc == null || acc.BillingCountry == oldAcc.BillingCountry) {
                continue;
            }
            newCountryByAccountId.put(acc.Id, acc.BillingCountry);
            oldCountryByAccountId.put(acc.Id, oldAcc.BillingCountry);
            if (String.isNotBlank(oldAcc.BillingCountry)) {
                oldCountries.add(oldAcc.BillingCountry);
            }
        }

        if (newCountryByAccountId.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // 2. One query, scoped to the changed accounts and pre-filtered to the
        //    candidate contacts (blank, or matching one of the old countries).
        //    Exact matching is re-checked in Apex below because SOQL text
        //    comparison is case-insensitive.
        List<Contact> candidates = [
            SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry
            FROM Contact
            WHERE AccountId IN :newCountryByAccountId.keySet()
              AND (MailingCountry = null OR MailingCountry IN :oldCountries)
        ];

        // 3. Decide per contact, no queries or DML inside the loop.
        List<Contact> toUpdate = new List<Contact>();
        for (Contact c : candidates) {
            String newCountry = newCountryByAccountId.get(c.AccountId);
            String oldCountry = oldCountryByAccountId.get(c.AccountId);
            String current = c.MailingCountry;

            Boolean isBlank = String.isBlank(current);

            // Contact works abroad (MailingCountry set to something other than the old billing country): leave alone.
            if (!isBlank && !current.equals(oldCountry)) {
                continue;
            }

            // Already in sync: nothing to do.
            Boolean alreadyInSync = (isBlank && String.isBlank(newCountry))
                || (!isBlank && current.equals(newCountry));
            if (alreadyInSync) {
                continue;
            }

            c.MailingCountry = newCountry;
            toUpdate.add(c);
        }

        // 4. Single DML statement.
        if (!toUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            update toUpdate;
        }
    }
}
```

**Why I changed the approach**

I did not load all contacts with `SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry FROM Contact`. With about 2.3 million contacts that query breaks in several ways:

- **Row limit:** A synchronous transaction can return at most 50,000 query rows. The unfiltered query would throw `System.LimitException` before any logic runs, so every MDM batch of account updates would fail.
- **Heap limit:** Even if the row limit were not hit, holding 2.3 million SObjects in a map would exceed the 6 MB synchronous heap limit.
- **Wasted work:** Loading the whole table to use a few hundred contacts is a full scan on every update.

The bulk-safe pattern is one query scoped to the accounts in the trigger batch. Here is what the new version does:

- **One query, no SOQL in loops:** The query is filtered by `AccountId IN` the changed accounts. It is still a single query, and it runs once per trigger invocation.
- **Pre-filtering in SOQL:** The `MailingCountry` condition means the query returns only the contacts that could change.
- **Only changed accounts:** Accounts whose BillingCountry did not change are skipped. If nothing changed, the handler returns before querying.
- **Exact comparison in Apex:** SOQL matching is case-insensitive, so the final "blank or equal to old country" check uses `String.equals` in Apex.
- **Skipping no-op updates:** Contacts already in sync are not updated, which avoids unnecessary contact trigger and workflow runs.
- **One DML statement:** Contacts are updated in a single `update` call.
- **`without sharing`:** A user who can edit an account but cannot see every contact would otherwise leave some contacts stale. This is a judgment call, so review it against your security model.

**Residual limits to be aware of**

- The 50,000 query-row limit applies per transaction. Typical accounts have few contacts and a batch of 200 accounts will fit. If some accounts have thousands of contacts, the batch could exceed the limit. In that case, move the work to Batch Apex.
- The 10,000 DML-rows limit also applies per transaction, with the same caveat.
- If an account's BillingCountry is cleared, contacts that followed the old value will have their MailingCountry cleared too. The spec says to copy the new BillingCountry, so I followed it. If you want blank billing to leave mailing countries alone, add a check on `newCountry` being non-blank.
- Deploying to production requires test coverage of at least 75%. The test class is not in the file list, so I did not include it.

