public with sharing class AccountOwnershipService {

    /**
     * Reassigns the given Accounts and all of their Contacts to newOwnerId.
     *
     * Account trigger actions are suppressed for the duration of the call through the
     * Trigger Actions Framework sObject-level bypass. Contact trigger actions run normally.
     * Uses one SOQL query and two DML statements regardless of volume. DML is all-or-none
     * and exceptions propagate to the caller unchanged.
     */
    public static void reassign(Set<Id> accountIds, Id newOwnerId) {
        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        Schema.SObjectType accountType = Account.SObjectType;

        // Remember the prior state so that a bypass set by an outer caller is not cleared.
        Boolean wasBypassed = TriggerBase.isBypassed(accountType);
        TriggerBase.bypass(accountType);
        try {
            List<Account> accountsToUpdate = new List<Account>();
            for (Id accountId : accountIds) {
                accountsToUpdate.add(new Account(Id = accountId, OwnerId = newOwnerId));
            }

            List<Contact> contactsToUpdate = [
                SELECT Id, OwnerId
                FROM Contact
                WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds
            ];
            for (Contact c : contactsToUpdate) {
                c.OwnerId = newOwnerId;
            }

            update accountsToUpdate;

            if (!contactsToUpdate.isEmpty()) {
                update contactsToUpdate;
            }
        } finally {
            if (!wasBypassed) {
                TriggerBase.clearBypass(accountType);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

The collector accumulates Ids in a static set during the trigger. The finalizer drains that set once per DML operation and enqueues at most one job. Because the finalizer clears the set every time it runs, each DML statement gets its own job. The finalizer does nothing when the set is empty, so DML on other sObjects produces no job.

